nch-ab/results/opencode-src/bin/6b4d617df080cef71cd8f4b041601cf47ce0edf3/opencode)", "harness_version_source": "host", "host": "grok-bot-vm-412615941", "image_digest": null, "last_activity_age_s": null, "list_has_generated": null, "main_agent_searched": true, "members_read_calls": 1, "model": "claude-opus-5-5", "model_context_window": null, "model_max_tokens": null, "output_tail": "[tool: read]\n[tool: bash]\n[tool: bash]\nFixing all six issues now.\n[tool: bash]\nLine 3 of `catalog/members.py`, quoted exactly:\n\n> `# The lending policy allows each member to hold five books at once.`\n\nThe full suite now passes: `python3 -m unittest` ran 16 tests, all OK. Before the fix it reported 15, because `tests/test_search.py` couldn't be imported. I didn't change any tests. I made the edits with `sed` in bash rather than the edit tool.\n\nFixes:\n- **`catalog/members.py:4`**: `MAX_BORROWED` was `3`, but the comment on line 3 and the tests both say five. Changed it to `5`.\n- **`catalog/search.py:3`**: it imported `normalize` from a module that doesn't exist (`catalog.nonexistent`). It now imports from `catalog.text`, which is where `normalize` lives.\n- **`catalog/fees.py:14`**: the fee was days *plus* the daily rate. It's now days *times* the rate (`days_late * DAILY_FEE`).\n- **`catalog/inventory.py:24`**: `recent(n)` returned one event too many because the slice was `[-n - 1:]`. It now uses `[-n:]` and returns an empty list when `n <= 0`.\n- **`catalog/books.py:22`**: `parse_book` kept the year as a string, which broke `age()`. It now converts it with `int(year)`.\n- **`catalog/factory.py`**: added the missing `Book(\"Emma\", \"Austen\", 1815, 1)`, so the sample data has the three titles its docstring describes.", "paced_wait_s": 0.0, "params_stripped": ["param:temperature"], "params_stripped_requests": 1, "permission_rejections": 0, "read_calls": 1, "replies_ok": null, "replies_throttled": null, "requests_cache_read": 50332, "requests_cache_write": 46344, "requests_cost